---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

const title =
  'What to Do After a Car Accident in California (Before Talking to Insurance)';
const description =
  'A step-by-step guide on what to do after a car accident in California, including medical care, documentation, and dealing with insurance before a lawsuit.';
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Header />

    <main class="article">
      <header class="article-header">
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          If you’ve been in a car accident in California, what you do in the first hours and days can
          quietly determine whether your injury claim succeeds or struggles. This guide explains the
          practical steps to take <strong>before</strong> talking to the insurance company.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>1. Make safety and medical care the priority</h2>
        <p>
          Your health comes first. If anyone is injured, call 911. Even if injuries seem minor,
          symptoms like concussions, soft-tissue injuries, and internal trauma often appear hours or
          days later.
        </p>
        <p>
          If emergency transport isn’t required, seek medical evaluation as soon as possible. From a
          claim perspective, delayed treatment is one of the most common reasons insurers question
          whether an injury was caused by the accident.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>2. Document the scene while it’s fresh</h2>
        <p>
          If it’s safe, take photos or video of the vehicles, license plates, damage, skid marks,
          traffic signals, and the surrounding area. Get contact information for witnesses.
        </p>
        <p>
          Police reports can be helpful, but they are not always complete. Your own documentation can
          become critical later.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>3. Be careful what you say at the scene</h2>
        <p>
          Stick to exchanging required information and checking on safety. Avoid speculating about
          fault or apologizing, even casually. Statements made in stressful moments are often taken
          out of context later.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>4. Notify insurance — but don’t rush into a statement</h2>
        <p>
          Most policies require prompt notice of an accident. That does <em>not</em> mean you must
          immediately give a recorded statement to the other driver’s insurance company.
        </p>
        <p>
          Insurance adjusters are trained to ask questions that lock people into incomplete or
          inaccurate descriptions before they fully understand their injuries.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>5. Follow through with treatment and keep records</h2>
        <p>
          Attend follow-up appointments, follow medical advice, and keep copies of records, bills,
          and prescriptions. Gaps in care are frequently used to argue that injuries were minor or
          unrelated.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>6. Understand that early offers are often incomplete</h2>
        <p>
          Quick settlement offers may seem helpful, especially when bills are arriving. In reality,
          they often come before the full extent of injuries and future treatment is known.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once a release is signed, you generally cannot go back — even if symptoms worsen.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="callout">
        <h2>When it makes sense to get guidance</h2>
        <p>
          You don’t need to file a lawsuit to get help understanding the process. Many injury claims
          are resolved pre-litigation, but they still benefit from clear documentation, timing, and
          strategy.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you’re injured and unsure what to do next, you can reach out for a no-pressure
          conversation about your situation.
        </p>
        <a href="/contact" class="cta">Get clear information about your injury</a>
      </section>

      <section class="disclaimer">
        <p>
          <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This article provides general information and does not constitute
          legal advice. Reading this does not create an attorney-client relationship.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <Footer />

    <style>
      .article {
        max-width: 800px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 3rem 1.25rem 4rem;
      }

      .article-header h1 {
        font-size: clamp(32px, 4vw, 44px);
        line-height: 1.15;
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
        letter-spacing: -0.02em;
      }

      .lede {
        font-size: 1.125rem;
        line-height: 1.7;
        opacity: 0.9;
        max-width: 70ch;
      }

      section {
        margin-top: 2.25rem;
      }

      h2 {
        font-size: 1.25rem;
        margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
      }

      p {
        line-height: 1.7;
        margin: 0.5rem 0;
      }

      .callout {
        margin-top: 3rem;
        padding: 1.5rem;
        border: 1px solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
        border-radius: 12px;
      }

      .cta {
        display: inline-block;
        margin-top: 1rem;
        padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
        border-radius: 10px;
        border: 1px solid currentColor;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-weight: 600;
      }

      .disclaimer {
        margin-top: 3rem;
        font-size: 0.85rem;
        opacity: 0.8;
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
